## Overview

This infrastructure hosts the website `www.foobar.com` using a scaled-up web infrastructure.

The main goal of this design is to improve availability, maintainability, and scalability by separating infrastructure components by role.

The infrastructure contains:

- 2 HAProxy load balancers configured as a cluster
- 1 dedicated web server running Nginx
- 1 dedicated application server running the application logic
- 1 dedicated MySQL database server

The user accesses the website through `www.foobar.com`. DNS resolves the domain name to a virtual IP address used by the load balancer cluster.

## Added Components

### Additional Server

An additional server is added to help scale the infrastructure and separate responsibilities.

Instead of running the web server, application server, and database on the same machine, the components are split across dedicated servers.

This improves:

- performance
- maintainability
- scalability
- security
- fault isolation

If one component needs more resources, it can be scaled or maintained independently.

### Second Load Balancer

A second HAProxy load balancer is added and configured as a cluster with the first one.

The goal is to remove the load balancer as a single point of failure.

If one load balancer fails, the other one can continue receiving traffic and forwarding requests to the infrastructure.

The two load balancers can share a virtual IP address. The active load balancer handles the traffic, and if it fails, the second load balancer takes over.

This setup improves high availability.

### Dedicated Web Server

A dedicated web server is added to handle HTTP or HTTPS requests.

The web server runs Nginx.

Its role is to:

- receive client requests from the load balancer
- serve static files when possible
- forward dynamic requests to the application server
- return responses to the client through the load balancer

By separating the web server from the application server and database, the web layer can be scaled independently.

### Dedicated Application Server

A dedicated application server is added to run the business logic of the website.

The application server processes dynamic requests received from Nginx.

Its role is to:

- execute the application logic
- use the application files
- process user actions
- communicate with the database when needed
- return generated responses to the web server

This separation makes the application easier to maintain and scale.

### Dedicated Database Server

A dedicated MySQL database server is added to store persistent data.

The database server stores structured data such as:

- users
- sessions
- products
- posts
- settings
- application data

The application server communicates with the database server when it needs to read or write data.

By isolating the database on its own server, the database can be optimized, secured, backed up, and scaled independently.

## Application Server vs Web Server

A web server and an application server do not have the same role.

### Web Server

The web server handles HTTP or HTTPS requests from users.

In this infrastructure, the web server is Nginx.

It is responsible for:

- receiving requests from the load balancer
- serving static content such as images, CSS, JavaScript files, or HTML files
- forwarding dynamic requests to the application server
- sending the final response back to the client

The web server is the entry point of the backend infrastructure.

### Application Server

The application server runs the application logic.

It is responsible for:

- executing the codebase
- processing dynamic requests
- applying business rules
- communicating with the database
- generating dynamic responses

For example, if a user logs in, creates an account, posts a comment, or searches for data, the application server handles the logic behind that action.

### Main Difference

The web server mainly handles HTTP traffic and static content.

The application server handles dynamic logic and business rules.

In simple terms:

- Nginx receives and routes the request
- The application server understands what the application must do
- MySQL stores and returns the data needed by the application

## Load Balancer Cluster

The two HAProxy load balancers are configured as a cluster.

A common approach is to use a virtual IP address shared between both load balancers.

One load balancer can be active while the other is passive.

If the active load balancer fails, the passive load balancer takes over the virtual IP address and continues routing traffic.

This removes the load balancer as a single point of failure.

## Request Flow

1. The user enters `www.foobar.com` in a browser.
2. DNS resolves `www.foobar.com` to the virtual IP address of the load balancer cluster.
3. The request reaches one of the HAProxy load balancers.
4. HAProxy forwards the request to the Nginx web server.
5. Nginx receives the HTTP or HTTPS request.
6. If the request is static, Nginx can serve the static content directly.
7. If the request is dynamic, Nginx forwards it to the application server.
8. The application server executes the application logic using the application files.
9. If data is required, the application server sends a query to the MySQL database server.
10. MySQL returns the requested data.
11. The application server generates a response.
12. The response goes back to Nginx.
13. Nginx returns the response through the load balancer.
14. The user receives the website response in the browser.

## Why This Infrastructure Is Better

This infrastructure is better than the previous ones because responsibilities are separated.

The load balancing layer handles traffic distribution and availability.

The web server layer handles HTTP or HTTPS requests.

The application server layer handles dynamic application logic.

The database layer handles persistent data.

This makes the infrastructure easier to scale, secure, monitor, and maintain.

## Remaining Limitations

Even if this infrastructure is better, it may still have limitations.

If there is only one web server, the web server is still a single point of failure.

If there is only one application server, the application server is still a single point of failure.

If there is only one database server, the database is still a single point of failure.

To improve this further, we could add:

- multiple web servers
- multiple application servers
- database replication
- automatic failover
- monitoring and alerting
- firewalls between layers
- HTTPS between internal services
- backups and disaster recovery